# mcp-git-ops — environment variables
#
# HOW THESE ARE LOADED. This server reads os.environ directly and does NOT load
# a .env file — there is no python-dotenv dependency and no load_dotenv() call
# in the repo. An MCP server receives its environment from the MCP client, so
# these belong in the client config: for Claude Code, ~/.claude/settings.json
# under mcpServers -> git-ops -> env. Copying this file to .env has no effect.
#
# No credentials are needed for normal use. As README.md states, the server
# uses the git credential store already configured on the host (SSH keys,
# credential helpers, or stored HTTPS tokens). GITHUB_TOKEN is deliberately NOT
# listed below: it is read by base/clients.py's GitHub client, and server.py
# imports only GitRepoClient, so no git_* tool ever reaches it.

# GIT_INSTALL_ROOT — optional. Read by hook_shell_fix.py, which server.py calls
# at import time. When locating the git executable it tries PATH first, then
# this, then the standard install locations. Useful on Windows when git is not
# on PATH. Was undocumented here.
GIT_INSTALL_ROOT=your_value_here

# GIT_DEFAULT_BRANCH — NOT IMPLEMENTED. Read by nothing.
#
# This is the one worth reading carefully, because README.md described a
# BEHAVIOUR rather than just a name: "Override the default base branch name
# used when from_branch is not specified". No code consults it.
# git_branch_create declares `from_branch: str = "main"` as a hard-coded
# literal default, so on a repository whose base branch is `master`, branch
# creation attempts to fetch a `main` that does not exist — with the override
# apparently set and silently doing nothing. Pass from_branch explicitly until
# this is implemented.
# GIT_DEFAULT_BRANCH=main

# GIT_REPO_PATH — NOT IMPLEMENTED. Read by nothing. Every tool takes a
# `repo_path` parameter that defaults to "." instead. Kept commented rather
# than deleted so the intent stays visible.
# GIT_REPO_PATH=/path/to/repo
